From b157cc7e7ef707671e02d2503aa2cd3cbb1f3c28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 11:58:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 160/180] hydra + kernel: Gaussian splats from USD, composited
 without noise

Houdini hands a UsdVol ParticleField3DGaussianSplat to a delegate as a
`particleField` rprim, which the delegate did not list: Hydra dropped the
splats without a word. It is now a point cloud rendered as Cycles 5.3
Gaussian splats, read from the primvars usdVolImaging publishes
(positions, scales, orientations, opacities, float or half, and
displayColor).

Cycles 5.3 hits a splat stochastically, with a probability of its opacity
times its response. For a faint glowing haze (opacity 0.01) that leaves a
relative noise of 10 per sample, and its bounding sphere dropped every
splat below an opacity of 0.01. So a second mode, the default for splats
without spherical harmonics (`cycles:gsplat:mode` = composite):

- splats are never hit. After each closest-hit query the integrator
  traces the segment up to the hit and composites every splat crossed,
  weighted-average order independent blending of response x linear
  colour, adds that as emission and multiplies the throughput by the
  segment's transmittance;
- Embree: composited splats carry only a dedicated mask bit, the
  composite query is an rtcOccluded1 with its own user geometry callback
  that accumulates and reports nothing;
- OptiX: their instance mask is the volume scatter bit (camera and bounce
  rays skip them in hardware); the composite traversal is told apart in
  __intersection__point by ray flags no other trace uses, with p4 = 0 so
  the any-hit programs ignore triangles and curves;
- other BVHs (CUDA without OptiX, HIP, Metal) do not draw them;
- bounding spheres go down to a response of 1e-4 (3 sigma at 0.01).

Trained splats, with harmonics, stay stochastic (the order 0 coefficient;
higher bands not read yet). Measured on the CPU against a numpy
reference of the same compositing, 96x64 at 16 spp: one isotropic splat,
one rotated anisotropic, three overlapping colours and a 400 splat
string all match within 0.1 % in mean and 1 % per 4x4 block, with no
noise.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
